 \paragraph{Contributions.}
 \begin{enumerate}[label=(\roman*),leftmargin=*,nosep]
 \item \textbf{Detection before construction, for different kinds of agent memory.}
 \sys{} configures a store's read path from two closed-form statistics of its bytes, with no model, labels, per-store queries or candidate runs. It re-derives that configuration in seconds as the store grows. The share of numbered lines tells whether the store records an order and only then turns on keep-newest resolution and parent-priority retrieval. The share of text in short records tells whether a finer cut keeps every record whole. The detected structure then sets how hybrid retrieval splits the slots among its channels, and assembly keeps stored order.
 \item \textbf{First place on MemoryAgentBench.}
-The released implementation with the paper settings scores $60.5$ against $43.6$ at the benchmark's GPT-4o-mini reader and leads the reported retrieval and memory systems on every competency. It scores $62.1$ and $67.0$ with GPT-4.1-mini and GPT-5-mini, and $69.7$ and $67.0$ with GLM-5.3-Flash and GPT-6 Luna. Per-store automatic configuration scores $58.5$ at GPT-4o-mini. Table~\ref{tab:board} retains the original implementation for comparison.\looseness=-1
-\item \textbf{The original implementation across three benchmarks.}
-On LoCoMo, \sys{} is the strongest row under both answer prompts ($93.46$ against $93.05$ under the strongest system's own) with no generative build or search call, whereas that system makes $9{,}398$. On LoCoMo-Refined it matches that system under the official evaluation ($77.57$ against $75.33$) and leads it by $8.5$ points with GLM-5.3-Flash. On BEAM, it matches the strongest system we compare at equal context from 100K to 10M tokens ($71.3$ against $70.8$ for the strongest model-built memory). It has the highest mean with the open-weight Qwen3.5-9B at 9.7K tokens. It builds a 10M-token memory in $77$ seconds instead of $13$ hours and $10{,}429$ calls.
-\item \textbf{The original implementation works with or without a model-built memory.}
